WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.613 --> 00:00:03.196
(gentle music)

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.670
My name is Joel Baker.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.380
I run American Giants.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.260
What we do is find and restore

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.890
old 1960s International Fiberglass giants.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.660
I'm gonna walk you through how my team

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.940
restores a muffler man.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.540
In this case, a Paul Bunyan.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.540
Muffler men were large
20-foot advertising giants

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.130
that were made in the early '60s

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.320
by the International Fiberglass Company.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.750
These guys were really
made to bring attention

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:31.583
to your business.

00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:34.560
So business owners love to
buy them, set them up outside,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.410
and it would get the family car
to pull over on a road trip.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.480
This particular muffler man was buried

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.330
in the back of an old
campground in Wisconsin

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.770
that had gone out of business.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.410
We're guessing he's around a 1964 model,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.503
which would make him kind of
one of the early ones made.

00:00:53.481 --> 00:00:55.339
When I first discovered this guy

00:00:55.339 --> 00:00:56.622
and got a good look at him,

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:58.200
I realized he had fallen over.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.392
When they fall forward
they land on their hands,

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:03.395
and that had pushed his
shoulders out and cracked them.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.300
Our guys hold the shoulder in place,

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.840
while another guy will screw small boards

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.810
all around the crack to hold it in place.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.020
And when we're happy with
that fit, then we screw it in,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.593
and the next step is we
go into fiberglass filler.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.660
Once the boards are holding
the shoulder in place,

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.220
for this particular crack,

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.740
then we can go on the inside of the giant

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.290
and we can lay down fresh fiberglass.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.080
and basically connect that shoulder piece

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.643
back to the rest of the giant.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.720
This Bunyan head had a lot
of detail in the beard.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.840
So we basically take Bondo
and fiberglass resin,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.263
and we put it into all
those seams of the beard.

00:01:54.105 --> 00:01:56.190
Once all the cracks on the giant are fixed

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.050
and all the holes are patched,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.000
it's really the hardest part of the job,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.950
and this is sanding.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.920
We use orbital sanders to sand the giant,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.010
and we go over every
square inch of the giant

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.290
and remove all those layers of paint

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.513
that had added up over the years.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.340
Once all the fiberglass
repair has been done,

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.560
then it's time for the
first coat of primer.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.710
And we use a filler primer that's thick,

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.020
and it can seep into all
these little pinholes

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.693
and cracks, and fill them as best it can.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:35.103
The primer is sprayed
onto the whole giant.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.030
Simultaneously, while all
this priming is going on,

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.060
we are also working on the feet.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.800
The major thing wrong with this guy

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.330
was he was missing his feet.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.760
And this is very common.
We see this a lot.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.160
When you have a business
that has a muffler man

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.330
and then they want to get rid of him,

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.190
instead of unbolting the feet,

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.830
they cut the feet off at the ankles.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.700
We have made molds of the
correct feet from other giants.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.683
We had to make new boots.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.420
Then we find a point on
the original giant's leg

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:10.530
that we can cut.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.080
And we basically cut about a foot off

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.000
of the original leg at a good
mating point for the new boot.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.320
We get a good lineup on the
boot, into the original leg.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.290
And then we use wood and screws

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.800
to basically bolt that
on and hold it in place.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.960
Once we're happy with the position

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.300
and that the toes are pointing
in the right direction

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.890
and everything looks good,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.000
then we're gonna go in.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.093
And we're gonna fiberglass
the inside of those legs.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.340
And then we'll come back,

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.470
and we'll actually prime that area again

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.720
and make sure it's a perfect fit.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.203
And you would never know that
this guy has replacement feet.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:51.653
In this case,

00:03:51.653 --> 00:03:54.690
the buyer also wanted an
axe that was an original.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.820
We didn't have an
original axe, so we again,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.760
used our molds to make an identical axe

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.530
to the original axes.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.510
Once the axe is put together,

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.173
then we go ahead and sand
it down and prime it.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.430
In anything the giants hold,

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.440
we always drill holes in them.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.223
That way if there are any water
leaks, the water can drain.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.250
The next step is very tedious,
but it's very important.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.140
And it's called wet sanding.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.360
Instead of using orbital sanders,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.120
we will use sandpaper by hand,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.810
and we will spray the giant
down, so it's all wet.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.620
And we'll use a very
fine grit of sandpaper

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.410
to go over that second coat of primer.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.170
And this is basically
getting rid of anything

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.500
in that primer paint
that's you can't even see

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:47.958
but that is sticking up

00:04:47.958 --> 00:04:50.449
and may make the final paint job

00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:52.293
look not quite as smooth.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:59.040
Once the wet sanding is complete

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.050
and everything looks good to us,

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.480
it's an exciting time
because we need to send

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:07.560
all of our pieces a few
miles away to a paint shop.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.670
Once at the paint shop,

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.790
the painter is going to
talk to us about what colors

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.560
we want for this giant.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:15.360
It's very important that the buyer picks

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.160
out what colors that they want.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.450
We often will recommend

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.160
that they go with the original colors

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.230
since we like everything to be original,

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.610
but it's really up to the buyer

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.770
and what paint job they want.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.450
The first thing the painter will do

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.790
is to put on the base coats

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.580
on the shoes, the legs,
the shirt, and the head.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.240
And these are all solid
colors that go on each parts.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.970
We always use automotive-quality paint

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.070
to give us a five- to seven-year life

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:44.520
before you see any signs of deterioration.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.010
Once the base coats are on the giant,

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.810
then it's time for the details.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.570
And this is when the
painter will go to work

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:55.200
with an airbrush and
do all the fine details

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.040
of highlighting the
hair, adding skin tones.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.910
The painter will also take
great care in doing the eyes

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.033
and adding a lot of eye
details in the pupils.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.300
Once all the airbrush
details are complete,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.730
then it is time for a clear coat.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.030
And this gives it a
really nice glossy shine.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:21.300
And this clear coat
also protects the giant

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.160
for the long term from sun

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.443
and weather and being in the elements.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.760
Once the pieces start
coming back to the shop

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:33.900
fully painted,

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.570
it's the time to really concentrate

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.600
on the internal structure of the giant.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.510
A lot of the buyers want
the giant to free-stand

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.340
in heavy, heavy wind.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.340
The first thing we do is
we do a cardboard cutout

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.050
of the giant's footprint

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.030
And then we take that cardboard cutout

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:54.600
to a machine shop, and they
make us a print of that foot

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.330
that's made out of 1/4-inch steel.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:01.110
Then we can weld on
4-inch pipes onto that.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.050
And those pipes will go up the leg.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.450
Once the pipes reach the waist,

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:10.713
we weld in cross bracing
that connects to the waist.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:15.000
It's during the process of
installing the internal structure

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.580
that we also bolt the giant together.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.760
We do a final fit of
the waist and the torso.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.523
And we also bolt on the head at this time.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.390
A date is agreed on with the buyer

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.700
of the giant, and before we get there,

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.790
a slab has been poured at that location.

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.950
Once the giant is unwrapped on-site,

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.830
it's time to install
anything that he needs

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.450
to hold in his hands.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:40.290
With this Bunyan,

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.060
he held an axe, so we made sure

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.860
that all the bolts that the axe

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.990
were gonna slide onto
were already in place.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:47.850
So on-site, it's pretty easy

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:50.160
to just make sure the fit is right

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.923
and bolt the axe down
before he's raised up.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.180
At this point, a crane
or a lift is brought in

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.050
to lift the giant from the trailer.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:02.850
We take time at this point to make sure

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:05.550
that the giant is orientated properly.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.710
We'll have the new buyer come in and look

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:10.560
at the giant and make
sure they are 100% happy

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.450
with the way the giant is facing.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.920
And when they are good,

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.920
we gently set the giant down on the slab,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.530
and those foot plates
that are under his feet,

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.830
they have tabs in them
and those tabs have holes.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.460
And we basically drill through
those holes into the slab.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.560
Once all the holes have been drilled,

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:31.530
we will insert concrete anchor bolts.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:33.750
And once we tighten those bolts up,

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.990
the bolts expand
underneath in the concrete

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:39.420
and press against the concrete

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:42.303
therefore locking the giant into place.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.350
Once the giant is standing,

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.620
we like to take a step
back and get some pictures

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:54.660
and really take the time to appreciate

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.880
all our hard work in bringing another one

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.680
of these giants back to life.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.840
These giants up to this point have lasted

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.390
for 50 years, and we
really wanna give them

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.340
another 50 years.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.440
And now this fully restored Bunyan

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.140
stands at Cumberland Ridge Forest Products

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.793
in Elkton, Kentucky.

